From 9cef65b10ccd8011210efcd6e2c832710bf2f7f3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: omesh <roland@nordhain.com>
Date: Sun, 19 Apr 2026 08:25:33 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 10/41] grpc: add tonic + prost deps + StreamBlocks proto +
 build hook (no service yet)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 1a step 1 of the gRPC streaming RPC for high-throughput wallet sync.
This commit only adds the infrastructure — proto definition, build-time
codegen, and dependency wiring. No service implementation, no server
spawn, no behavioural change. `cargo check -p cuprated` passes, generated
bindings land at $OUT_DIR/cuprate.stream.v1.rs.

Why streaming RPC at all: bin RPC over HTTP/1.1 caps wallet sync at
~12.6 MB/s (50 % of a 25 MB/s link) due to per-TCP-connection variance
across the 16 parallel pull clients — slowest connection of the batch
gates each iteration. HTTP/2 multiplexing on a single connection plus
server-streaming removes both the variance ceiling and the per-batch
round-trip wait. Realistic estimate: 1.6–1.8x more throughput on the
network layer, ~3 min Ledger restore down to ~2 min.

Why side-by-side with bin RPC, not a replacement: standard wallets keep
working unchanged. Only "smart" wallets that opt into the streaming
endpoint get the win. No risk to existing setups.

Proto design notes (proto/cuprate_stream.proto):
- BlockChunk.blocks[i] is the same epee-serialized block_complete_entry
  the bin RPC produces, so the wallet reuses its existing parser — no
  second deserializer needed.
- chain_tip travels in every chunk so the wallet updates its progress
  bar without a separate get_info round-trip (the same get_info call
  that today blocks for 12 s behind in-flight 50 MB block responses).
- client_request_id / server_request_id / chunk_seq are baked in from
  day one so cross-machine correlation works without later patches.

Build dep: protoc on PATH. macOS `brew install protobuf`, Linux
`apt install protobuf-compiler`. Already installed on Roland's macOS dev
box (libprotoc 34.1).
